        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 7,
        "titulo": "Word order and your first questions",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: listen to «Ich heiße Lea.» and then «Wie heißt du?». To say your name, the verb is «heißen», \"to be called\" — German says \"I am called Lea\", not \"my name is\". STEP 2: in German, the conjugated verb is always in SECOND position in the sentence. In «Ich heiße Lea», «ich» takes first position and «heiße» second. STEP 3: in questions with a question word, that word comes first. Examples: «wie», which means \"how\" and sounds like «vee», and «woher», which means \"where from\" and sounds like «voh-HEHR». The verb stays second: «Wie heißt du?» and «Woher kommst du?». STEP 4: in a yes/no question, the verb moves to the front: «Heißt du Lea?» — no \"do\" needed, unlike English. Answer «Ja» or «Nein». STEP 5: the verb ending changes with the person. With «ich», it ends in «e»: «ich heiße», «ich komme». With «du», it ends in «st»: «du kommst». In «heißen», the «ß» already makes the «s» sound, so you only add «t»: «du heißt». STEP 6: as in English, the pronoun is required: «Ich komme aus den USA», never just «Komme aus den USA». STEP 7: a heads-up. In «der Name» and «der Vorname», «der» is an article (a German \"the\"). You'll learn the articles in lesson 3. For now, repeat «der» together with the word.",
        "comparacao": "English and German start out alike: «Ich heiße Lea», \"I am called Lea\". The difference shows up as soon as something else opens the sentence. English keeps subject-then-verb (\"Today I come\"), but German keeps the verb in second place no matter what comes first: «Heute komme ich», literally \"Today come I\". English has a trace of this in questions (\"Where are you from?\"), and German questions work the same way: «Woher kommst du?». One more trap: «Wer» means \"who\", not \"where\" — \"where\" is «wo».",
